# Line-ending and binary-safety rules for <%PROJECT_SLUG%>.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every development operation runs through a
# code/src/scripts/**/*.sh script, and the supported Windows path is WSL 2
# (README.md -> Supported platforms). A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites those scripts to CRLF, and the shebang then fails with an error that
# names neither the file nor the cause:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# Inside a container the same defect is worse, because it is not even reported at
# the point of failure: the Django images COPY code/src/docker/django/entrypoint.*.sh
# and exec them, so a CRLF checkout builds an image that will not start.
#
# Normalising here removes the whole class on every clone, without asking anyone to
# configure git correctly first. Measured 16/08/2026: no tracked file contained a
# CRLF when these rules were added, so they pin the status quo rather than rewrite it.

# ── Text ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
# `eol=lf` is stated rather than left to each developer's core.autocrlf because
# nothing here wants CRLF: there is no .bat, .cmd or .ps1 in the tree, and the
# shells, containers and interpreters that read these files are all POSIX.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated again for the files where a CRLF is a failure rather than a cosmetic
# difference — a shebang, a container entrypoint, an interpreter directive, or a
# value docker compose reads literally. Redundant against the rule above by design:
# these are the lines someone must consciously delete to break the build.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.rs          text eol=lf
*.toml        text eol=lf
*.conf        text eol=lf
*.example     text eol=lf
Dockerfile*   text eol=lf
.python-version text eol=lf
.nvmrc        text eol=lf

# ── Binary ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never normalised, never diffed as text. No binary file is tracked today; these
# rules exist so the first design asset committed under
# project-management/src/00-ASSETS/ cannot be corrupted by the `text=auto` guess.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
